# 11. INTERNAL TRANSFERS AND FAMILY FUND ACCOUNTING

This is one of the most important parts of the application.

The app must distinguish between:
1. External income
2. External expense
3. Internal transfer between cash/bank accounts
4. Family member contribution to a common fund
5. Savings allocation
6. Opening balance

These concepts must not be combined into a single generic transaction.

## 11.1 Common family fund

The family uses a common fund.

When Papa receives salary into the family's bank account, record actual income once.

When money is moved from the bank account to a cash wallet, record an internal transfer. Do not count this as income or expense.

When the family uses that cash to purchase groceries, record one grocery expense. Do not record another expense merely because the cash was withdrawn.

## 11.2 Contributions

If the app tracks how much each member contributed to the common fund, use a contribution ledger or clearly defined internal allocation records.

Contributions between a member's tracked funds and the family fund must not be counted as new external income if that money has already been recorded as income.

If money moves between tracked accounts, total combined cash/bank assets must remain unchanged.

## 11.3 Shared expenses

Every expense must have a single canonical transaction record.

The same transaction may appear in:
- The family expense total
- A category report
- A member-attribution report
- A vehicle report

However, those are different views of the same transaction and must never be added together to produce a grand total.

# 12. CASH, BANK BALANCES AND FINANCIAL FORMULAS

Implement a dedicated and testable financial calculation layer.

All monetary values must use exact decimal arithmetic, preferably integer paise stored in a 64-bit integer or another appropriately precise representation. Do not use binary floating-point arithmetic for money.

## 12.1 Actual income

For a reporting period:

Actual income = Sum of confirmed external income transactions in the period.

Exclude:
- Opening balances
- Internal transfers
- Contributions that merely move previously recorded money
- Expected but unreceived income

## 12.2 Actual expenses

Actual expenses = Sum of confirmed external expense transactions in the period, counted once per transaction.

Exclude:
- Internal transfers
- Savings allocations between tracked accounts
- Opening balances
- Unpaid scheduled bills

Loan principal and debt repayment tracking are not required in the current scope. If such features are added in the future, they must be modeled separately to avoid counting principal repayments as ordinary consumption and must not be silently included in the current application.

## 12.3 Monthly net cash flow

Monthly net cash flow = Actual income − Actual expenses.

A positive value indicates a surplus during the period. A negative value indicates a deficit during the period.

## 12.4 Monthly savings rate

If actual income is greater than zero:

Savings rate (%) = (Actual income − Actual expenses) ÷ Actual income × 100.

If actual income is zero, show “N/A” instead of dividing by zero.

A negative savings rate is valid and indicates that expenses exceeded income.

Use the label “Net savings / surplus” for the income-minus-expense figure to distinguish it from the family's total accumulated savings.

## 12.5 Account balances

For each tracked account:

Current balance = Opening balance + Confirmed credits − Confirmed debits.

Transfers must debit the source account and credit the destination account in one atomic operation.

The combined total of all tracked accounts must not change as a result of an internal transfer.

Do not treat monthly income minus monthly expenses as equivalent to the current cash/bank balance.

## 12.6 Total available balance

Total available balance = Sum of current balances of all tracked cash wallets and bank accounts.

If credit-card balances or other liabilities are not tracked, do not claim that this figure represents total net worth.

If the admin has not entered all relevant cash and bank accounts, label the figure as the “Recorded available balance” to make its limitations clear.

## 12.7 Budget utilization

For a budget with a positive limit:

Budget utilization (%) = Actual eligible expenses in the budget period ÷ Budget limit × 100.

Remaining budget = Budget limit − Actual eligible expenses.

If the remaining budget is negative, show the overspend amount.

If the budget limit is zero, display an appropriate “No budget set” or “Budget not configured” state rather than dividing by zero.

Family, member, and category budgets must not be added together to produce an expense total.

## 12.8 Vehicle expense formula

Vehicle monthly total = Sum of all confirmed expense transactions linked to that vehicle during the month.

Petrol, maintenance, insurance, PUC, and repairs are included once each.

## 12.9 Opening balances and reporting periods

Opening balances establish account state and must never appear as current-period income.

The default reporting period is the calendar month, from the first day through the last day of the month.

Support year selection and historical monthly reports.

Use consistent local date/time handling and test month boundaries, year boundaries, and leap years.

