# 19. BACKUP AND RESTORE

Implement both automatic local backup and manual backup.

This application is local-only, so automatic backup must not silently mean cloud synchronization.

## 19.1 Automatic local backup

Provide a configurable local backup schedule using Android-compatible background work scheduling.

Requirements:
- Run only when appropriate system conditions permit.
- Use atomic file creation and replacement.
- Avoid corrupting the previous valid backup if a new backup fails.
- Keep a configurable number of recent backups.
- Show the last successful backup time.
- Show an error if backup creation fails.
- Never claim a backup succeeded until it has actually been written and verified.

Explain that an automatic backup stored on the same phone may be lost if the device is lost, damaged, or reset.

## 19.2 Manual backup

Allow the admin to create a backup file using the Android Storage Access Framework.

The backup should include:
- Family member profiles
- Opening balances
- Account records
- Income and expense transactions
- Internal transfers
- Budgets
- Savings goals
- Recurring bill schedules
- Vehicle records
- App preferences that are safe to export
- Required schema/version metadata

Use a documented versioned format, such as a structured JSON backup with integrity verification. Encrypt sensitive backup contents if feasible and provide a usable recovery method.

## 19.3 Restore

Allow restoration from a supported backup file.

Before restoring:
- Validate the file format and version.
- Verify its integrity.
- Reject invalid or corrupted backups.
- Show the user what will be restored.
- Warn if current data will be replaced or merged.
- Require explicit confirmation.

The initial implementation may use a full-replacement restore to avoid ambiguous merges. If merge functionality is implemented, use stable transaction IDs and explicit duplicate handling.

A failed restore must not leave the database partially overwritten.

Test backup and restore using realistic records and verify that transaction totals and account balances remain unchanged after restoration.

